The SDL application entry always enabled DD2_REALTIME, so a real native
window/intro/frontend run with recorded original GetTickCount inputs exited
at its first engine clock call. Select the normal live clock only when no
recorded game clock was supplied. Keep explicit incompatible DD2_REALTIME
settings visible to the strict clock reader. Device timing remains a separate
input; this does not supply sound controls or game state.

--- a/dd2_native.c
+++ b/dd2_native.c
@@ -113,7 +113,10 @@
 void dd2_native_init(void){
     int index;
     if(!getenv("DD2_WINDOW"))return;
-    setenv("DD2_SOUND","1",1);setenv("DD2_REALTIME","1",1);
+    setenv("DD2_SOUND","1",1);
+    /* Recorded engine clock inputs also work with real SDL output. Keep
+       the live clock as the default for ordinary interactive startup. */
+    if(!getenv("DD2_TICK_REPLAY"))setenv("DD2_REALTIME","1",1);
     if(!getenv("DD2_LEVEL") && !getenv("DD2_PLAY") && !getenv("DD2_CHAMP"))setenv("DD2_FE","1",1);
     /* Preserve Unix termination signals. SDL otherwise turns SIGTERM/SIGINT
      * into the WM_CLOSE request that the original window procedure ignores. */
